jess: FYI customer acct_42 opened a ticket — create_order function calls failing with 400 invalid_tool_arguments when using JSON-mode + function-calling. They say same call used to work, now model is returning args as a string (double-encoded).
support-bot: ticket TCK-1984 created :ticket:
kai: can you paste the raw model response and server error lines? headers would help too.
jess: pasted below (redacted keys). Model reply chunk: 
```json
{
  "tool_call": {
    "name": "create_order",
    "arguments": "{\"items\":[{\"sku\":\"LAT-100\",\"qty\":2}],\"shipping\":{\"address\":\"123 Main St\"}}"
  }
}
```
Server log: 
```
ERROR [api.handler] parse_tool_args: expected JSON object for tool_call.arguments, got string
Trace: bad_tool_args: create_order - tool parsing failed
HTTP 400 - invalid_tool_arguments
```
ryan: that definitely looks like the model returned the arguments as an escaped JSON string rather than an object. Two likely causes: prompt/template told it to stringify, or the structured-output postprocessor accidentally stringified the args. Is this only on redwood-open-7b-v1 or across models?
eva: I checked recent deploys — we changed the functions guidance for smaller models to reduce hallucinations, there was a fallback that wraps args in quotes if the model returns text. Might be a regression where we always stringify before returning to the API.
kai: repro steps (I can reproduce locally):
```bash
curl -s -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer REDACTED' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model":"redwood-open-7b-v1",
    "messages":[{"role":"user","content":"create order with sku LAT-100 qty 2"}],
    "functions":[{"name":"create_order","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"items":{"type":"array"}},"required":["items"]}}],
    "response_mode":"json"
  }' | jq '.'
```
and sometimes the tool_call.arguments field is a string (escaped JSON).
ryan: OK — immediate mitigation for customer: server-side accept either object or string for tool args. If string, attempt JSON.parse after unwrapping quotes and trimming. That should unblock them quickly. I'll draft a small patch: 
```js
// in tool-call handler
let args = resp.tool_call.arguments;
if (typeof args === 'string') {
  try {
    args = JSON.parse(args);
  } catch (err) {
    // try forgiving unescape
    const maybe = args.replace(/^"|"$/g, '').replace(/\"/g, '"');
    try { args = JSON.parse(maybe); } catch (e) { throw new Error('invalid_tool_arguments'); }
  }
}
// then validate args against declared schema
validate(args, funcSchema);
```
jess: can we scope that hotfix to the customer's org only? They run on Dedicated but behind our control plane.
eva: yes, we can feature-flag it per-customer in the routing layer. I'll prepare a patch and push to staging. Also adding an automated test in the eval harness to feed both object and string-encoded args so we don't regress.
kai: long-term we should fix the generator/postprocessor so it returns native JSON objects for tool args when schema says object. I'll open a task for model-team + runtime: check prompt fragments and the codepath that serializes tool outputs.
ryan: customer comms: recommend they upgrade to SDK v0.9.3 which includes tolerant parsing on the client, or we can flip the org-scoped hotfix now. ETA for hotfix to staging: 1h, production: after quick QA 3-4h.
jess: I'll reply to customer with workaround + ask for a copy of their last failing request (we need exact request id). Also asking if they saw any errors on their side (e.g., double-stringifying before sending).
support-bot: TCK-1984 updated — hotfix scheduled to staging (job 4821).
